The Newton filtration of the Brieskorn lattice is defined by
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{N}_\beta G_0&= \operatorname{image}\bigl[\mathcal{N}_\beta\Omega^d(U)[u]\hookrightarrow\Omega^d(U)[u] \rightarrow G_0\bigr]\\
&=\mathcal{N}_\beta\Omega^d(U)[u]\big/\bigl((u\mathrm{d}+\mathrm{d} f)\Omega^{d-1}(U)[u]\cap \mathcal{N}_\beta\Omega^d(U)[u]\bigr).
\end{align*}

We only indicate the main ideas of the proof, showing the interplay between algebraic geometry and singularity theory.
\begin{enumerate}
\par\setbox0=\hbox{--\,--\,--\enspace} 
                  \hangindent\wd0\relax
From Kouchnirenko's formula, $\mu(f_{\boldsymbol{a}})$ is independent of $\boldsymbol{a}$.
\par\setbox0=\hbox{--\,--\,--\enspace} 
                  \hangindent\wd0\relax
A first step is to show that, separately, the left-hand side and the right-hand side of \eqref{eq:KKP} are independent of $\boldsymbol{a}$ as long as no entry of $\boldsymbol{a}$ vanishes. For the left-hand side, according to \eqref{eq:spFirr}, one can argue with the spectrum, and one shows that the spectrum at infinity of $f_{\boldsymbol{a}}$ is independent of $\boldsymbol{a}$, due to the semi-continuity property of \cite{N-S97} and the constancy of $\mu(f_{\boldsymbol{a}})$. For the right-hand side, one shows that the family $\mathrm{H}^d_{{\mathrm{dR}}}(U,f_{\boldsymbol{a}}/u)$ is an algebraic vector bundle on $\mathbb{G}_\mathrm{m}\times(\mathbb{G}_\mathrm{m})^{V(\Delta^{\scriptscriptstyle\vee})}$ with a flat connection having regular singularities along $\{0\}\times(\mathbb{G}_\mathrm{m})^{V(\Delta^{\scriptscriptstyle\vee})}$.
\par\setbox0=\hbox{--\,--\,--\enspace} 
                  \hangindent\wd0\relax
The second step is to prove the result when $a_v=1$ for all $v\in V(\Delta^*)$. We set $f=f_\mathbf{1}$. One knows from \cite{Batyrev94} that $X$ is a smooth Fano projective variety (i.e.,\  the canonical bundle $K_X$ is anti-ample), and (see\kern.3em\cite{Fulton93}), one can interpret $f$ as the first Chern class $c_1(TX)$ of $X$. In particular, by anti-ampleness of $K_X$, the cup product with $c_1(TX)$ satisfies the Hard Lefschetz property on the $\mathbb{Q}$-Chow ring of $X$. Due to a formula of Borisov-Chen-Smith \cite{B-C-S05}, this Chow ring is identified with the graded ring with respect to the Newton filtration:
\[
\mathrm{gr}_{\scriptscriptstyle\bullet}^\mathcal{N}(\mathbb{Q}[x,x^{-1}]/(\partial f).
\]
Then one identifies multiplication by $c_1(TX)=f$ on this ring tensored by $\mathbb{C}$ with the action of the monodromy on $\mathrm{H}^d_{\mathrm{dR}}(U,f)$, by an argument similar to that used by Varchenko \cite{Varchenko81}. The Hard Lefschetz property implies then the desired equality~\eqref{eq:KKP}.
\end{enumerate}
